I would just like to add my two cents worth (OK, maybe $1.02).  
I am perhaps a hack (yes, hack) fundamentalist, I even set up my
version to run with the hjkl cursor motion ala vi (UNIX user-unfriendly
editor) and got rid of anything resembling lines and such for
wall borders, preferring the old rogue display.  I first got
nethack 3.0.10 two weeks ago and have been amazed with it,
I love it.  I don't think a lot of the discussion about unfair
deaths has much value, though: I refuse to play explore mode and
to this day am an explore mode virgin and proud of it.  What's 
the point, if you have a problem with your characters dying, play in
wizard/debug mode, that's fine, but why complain that explore mode
(where your character is allowed to die) allows your character to die?

I think one of the wonderful things about nethack is the fact that
it has been developed by hundreds of people and part of the philosophy
is not to remove something that someone else wrote unless it doesn't work.
I have not had a problem with walking over a cockatrice corpse (for example)
since that happened to me a couple times ( I used to play autopickup, 
easy solution: type 'm (direction)' and look at what's there.  About blind
people ( in nethack ) feeling the ground as they move, that's part of the 
peril in being blind, maybe it could be alleviated to a certain extent,
but try eating a floating eye, then when you are blinded ( I believe )
corpses show up as well as living monsters (ESP, perhaps a bug? do dead
monsters really have mental activity?)

Elbereth abuse and the black pudding sink scam don't strike me as 
very real gripes against the game.  I tried it out in wizard mode
(I have only played one wizard-mode game to date, and intend to use 
it for maintenance as it was intended) and found that those pesky 
little puddings had no trouble killing me (Die y/n?) several times
each kick.  In order for this really to be "abuse" you have to 
be cheating (ie xplor or debug) anyway.  Polypiling, same thing.

The spoilers are not really the undoing of the game that they warn
they may be.  Playing in regular mode and trying to exploit them
as much as possible, the game still ends up being mostly HACK and 
slash.  In other words they are not really going to change the 
outcome of your average game.  If you really insist that you should 
be able to _win_ your average game: debug, ok?

To make a long (pardon) story short: leave nethack alone if you 
don't have anything to add. (some of the solutions to the cockatrice
problem are really novel and meritorious, saying the game is unfair
because it killed off your supercharacter - however arbitrarily - is
a little silly.  It often amuses me when I find a new pitfall. )

